  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.$",
  "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{\u2032}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+6x)}^{\u2032}{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} \\\\  & =(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+6 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-3.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-3.$",
